# generation: 1
# fleet.tsv - the fleet map. One line per container. ketsync decides placement;
# tp moves the data. This is NOT one of tp's work lists: engines/tp has its own
# inventory-migrate.tsv and inventory-replica.tsv, with different columns.
#
#   ct     the container id on its home node. The PRODUCTION id, and the only
#          id written down anywhere. The copy on the backup node and the
#          temporary one on a compute node are worked out from it - see below
#   tier   which copy pool it belongs in: ssd (comes back fast) or hdd.
#          Read by nothing today: it is a note to the human. The pool that
#          actually decides is the dest column of inventory-replica.tsv
#   home   the node it normally RUNS on, by IP. That is a compute node, not
#          the storage node - the storage node serves the disk over NFS, it
#          does not run the container. Find it with:
#              ls /etc/pve/nodes/*/lxc/<ct>.conf
#   dr     the node that picks it up when the storage node is gone, by IP.
#          Usually the same compute node: what died is the disk, not the CPU,
#          so the container comes back on the machine it was already on, using
#          that machine's own storage instead of NFS
#   dst    which storage on the dr node to put it on: local-lvm, local-zfs,
#          local... Leave it out and DR_DST from ctrep.conf is used, which
#          defaults to local-lvm. ct-distribute.sh handles lvmthin, lvm, dir,
#          nfs and zfspool, and refuses anything else by name rather than
#          guessing what shape it is
#
# THE THREE IDS, and why only one of them is in this file:
#
#   300    production. The raw image lives on the storage node and the compute
#          node NFS-mounts it. This is what goes in the ct column
#   8300   the copy on the backup node, written every replica round. 300 plus
#          OFFSET, which is 8000 in ctrep.conf
#   9300   the temporary container ct-distribute.sh makes on a compute node's
#          own storage during a disaster. 300 plus DR_OFFSET, which is 9000
#
# Nobody types 8300 or 9300. They are a policy in one config file rather than
# data in a table, so the numbering cannot drift per container - and seeing a
# 9 in front in `pct list` tells you without asking that it is temporary, its
# data is not replicated anywhere, and somebody has to unwind it deliberately.
#
# Addresses, not names - every address here must have a row in nodes.tsv, and
# 'ketsync doctor' refuses to stay quiet about one that does not.
#
# dr is written down rather than chosen at the time. Placing a customer by
# free RAM at 3am is how a customer ends up on the wrong machine; the same
# rule as tp's "no defaults, never guess". Override on the day with
# 'ketsync distribute --ctid <id> --to <ip>' and '--dst <storage>'.
#
# TAB separated; lines starting with # are ignored.
#
# ct	tier	home	dr	dst
110	hdd	10.100.1.32	10.100.1.33	local-lvm
120	hdd	10.100.1.33	10.100.1.32	local-lvm
# comes back on the machine it already runs on, onto that machine's own disk
300	hdd	10.100.1.33	10.100.1.33	local-lvm
# no dst: DR_DST from ctrep.conf decides
301	ssd	10.100.1.32	10.100.1.32
